    "q-94602": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94602",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the center of the elliptic cone $7{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=21{z}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(x-0)^{2}+3(y-0)^{2} & =21(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-0)^{2}}{1/7}+\\frac{(y-0)^{2}}{1/3} & =\\frac{(z-0)^{2}}{1/21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(0,0,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-94602-a",
