    "q-94485": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94485",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8}=\\frac{(z+6)^{2}}{6},$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the elliptic cone, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-6)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{(0+6)^{2}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{36}{9}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{36}{6} \\\\ 4+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{8} & =2 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =4\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =0,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(0,8,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
