      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the center of the elliptic cone $2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}={z}^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of an elliptic cone with center at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=\\frac{(z-{z}_{0})^{2}}{{c}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the center, we rewrite our equation in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{1/3} & =(z-0)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic cone that has its center at $(-2,1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
