    "q-35309": {
      "dom_id": "step-q35309",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Given that the intersection of the elliptic cone  ${x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the plane $z=2$ is an ellipse, what is the length of its minor axis?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the intersection of the elliptic cone and the plane $z=2,$ we substitute $z=2$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with a semi-minor axis of length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the minor axis has length $2b=2\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
