    "q-35265": {
      "dom_id": "step-q35265",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Given that the intersection of the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}={z}^{2}$ and the plane $z=3$ is an ellipse, what is the length of its minor axis?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the intersection of the elliptic cone and the plane $z=3,$ we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3}={z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{3} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{81}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{27} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{9}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with a semi-minor axis of length $b=3\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore, the minor axis has length $2b=6\\sqrt{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
